{"translation": "[3.2.6] The pseudodipteros is laid out so that there are eight columns each in the front and in the posticum, and fifteen each on the sides, counting the corner columns. The walls of the cella are to stand opposite the four middle columns in the front and in the posticum. Thus there will be a space of two intercolumniations plus the thickness of a column at its base, from the walls round to the outermost rows of columns. There is no example of this at Rome, but there is one at Magnesia, the temple of Diana by Hermogenes of Alabanda, and the temple of Apollo built by Menesthes. [3.2.7] The dipteros is octastyle both in the pronaos and in the posticum, but around the temple it has double rows of columns, as is the Doric temple of Quirinus and the Ionic temple of Diana at Ephesus, set up by Chersiphron. [3.2.8] The hypaethros is decastyle in the pronaos and in the posticum. In all other respects it has the same features as the dipteros, but in the interior it has columns doubled in height, that is in two tiers, set back from the walls so as to allow a passage round, like the porticoes of peristyles. The middle is open to the sky, without a roof. There are entrances with folding doors at either end, in the pronaos and in the posticum. Of this too there is no example at Rome, but at Athens there is the octastyle in the Olympian temple precinct.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 15, "surface": "angularibus", "english": "corner columns", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 25, "surface": "medianas", "english": "middle", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 32, "surface": "intercolumniorum", "english": "intercolumniations", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 63, "surface": "octastylos", "english": "octastyle", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 89, "surface": "decastylos", "english": "decastyle", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 92, "surface": "pronao", "english": "pronaos", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 100, "surface": "dipteros,", "english": "dipteros", "uncertain":